1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,691
Here is a question for a model:
what cheese is in this photo?

2
00:00:04,011 --> 00:00:09,323
Both answers on screen come
from the same predict code in cli.py.

3
00:00:09,643 --> 00:00:11,307
And both answers say brie.

4
00:00:11,550 --> 00:00:12,937
Now count the numbers.

5
00:00:13,257 --> 00:00:16,115
The top answer,
the README output, holds three.

6
00:00:16,435 --> 00:00:19,145
The bottom one,
from this recording, holds four.

7
00:00:19,465 --> 00:00:21,321
One model cannot give both.

8
00:00:21,641 --> 00:00:25,246
So which model is behind an endpoint,
and what else is there?

9
00:00:25,483 --> 00:00:29,581
Behind an endpoint sit three pieces:
an artifact, which model;

10
00:00:29,581 --> 00:00:34,208
a container, what runs it;
and a machine, where it runs, and who pays.

11
00:00:34,528 --> 00:00:38,326
The meter beside the machine keeps running
while no request arrives.

12
00:00:39,733 --> 00:00:42,805
In part 2,
we stop running these steps by hand.

13
00:00:43,125 --> 00:00:46,490
The four stages of the Cheese App
become one pipeline

14
00:00:46,490 --> 00:00:48,267
that the cloud runs for you.

15
00:00:48,517 --> 00:00:52,748
To follow along,
you need an AWS account with billing,

16
00:00:52,748 --> 00:00:56,666
the AWS CLI, Docker,
and both repositories cloned.

17
00:00:56,986 --> 00:00:59,631
This is our first lecture on AWS.

18
00:00:59,867 --> 00:01:02,701
A tracker in the corner
names the README step,

19
00:01:02,701 --> 00:01:05,904
and a yellow tag,
your turn, means pause and run it.

20
00:01:06,224 --> 00:01:08,763
Every command is also in the study guide.

21
00:01:10,100 --> 00:01:11,892
First, where this lecture sits.

22
00:01:12,212 --> 00:01:14,855
The course project
is in its prototype phase,

23
00:01:14,855 --> 00:01:18,143
and of the four parts of the Cheese App,
three are done.

24
00:01:18,463 --> 00:01:20,297
Model deploy is still open.

25
00:01:20,533 --> 00:01:22,816
Part 1 of this video fills that tile.

26
00:01:23,136 --> 00:01:24,864
Part 2 connects all four.

27
00:01:25,100 --> 00:01:28,769
Last week,
lecture 14 put containers on Cloud Run.

28
00:01:29,089 --> 00:01:31,713
Today, the container serves a trained model.

29
00:01:31,950 --> 00:01:35,044
The lecture maps its console
onto the course:

30
00:01:35,044 --> 00:01:37,520
notebooks, training from lecture 13,

31
00:01:37,520 --> 00:01:40,889
and today's two,
online prediction and pipelines.

32
00:01:41,133 --> 00:01:44,674
The lecture is written
for Vertex AI on Google Cloud.

33
00:01:44,994 --> 00:01:49,036
This course also ships an AWS
version of both tutorials,

34
00:01:49,036 --> 00:01:51,202
and that is the one we follow.

35
00:01:51,522 --> 00:01:53,442
Same ideas, different names.

36
00:01:53,762 --> 00:01:55,768
I'll point out each one as we meet it.

37
00:01:56,017 --> 00:01:58,342
A Google Cloud bucket becomes S3.

38
00:01:58,662 --> 00:02:03,264
A service account key becomes an IAM user
with a key on your laptop,

39
00:02:03,264 --> 00:02:04,956
plus a role in the cloud.

40
00:02:05,276 --> 00:02:09,030
And an endpoint becomes
a configuration plus an endpoint.

41
00:02:09,267 --> 00:02:13,342
One thing about this recording:
it has no AWS account.

42
00:02:13,662 --> 00:02:16,392
Every step that runs on your laptop is real.

43
00:02:16,712 --> 00:02:21,150
Every AWS call stops
at its honest first error, like this one.

44
00:02:21,470 --> 00:02:23,184
Where the cloud would answer,

45
00:02:23,184 --> 00:02:26,376
you see the README output,
or a tagged local stand-in.

46
00:02:26,696 --> 00:02:28,232
Never a faked success.

47
00:02:28,467 --> 00:02:31,219
Versions at the time
of recording are on screen.

48
00:02:33,367 --> 00:02:35,756
Step 1: clone the repository.

49
00:02:36,076 --> 00:02:40,172
The README says clone or download,
so our git clone is extra,

50
00:02:40,172 --> 00:02:43,799
and so is the git log
that shows the commit we follow.

51
00:02:44,119 --> 00:02:45,378
Pause and clone it.

52
00:02:45,617 --> 00:02:49,522
An extra listing:
the CLI, a Dockerfile, a Pipfile,

53
00:02:49,522 --> 00:02:54,193
eight sample photos,
and docker-shell.sh, already executable.

54
00:02:54,434 --> 00:02:57,591
Steps 2 and 3 are in the AWS console.

55
00:02:57,911 --> 00:03:01,474
Check that you can use
S3, SageMaker and IAM.

56
00:03:01,794 --> 00:03:06,615
Then create an IAM user called
model-deployment, with three policies.

57
00:03:06,935 --> 00:03:09,154
The third is broader than this code needs.

58
00:03:09,474 --> 00:03:11,607
Create access keys and save both.

59
00:03:12,150 --> 00:03:16,250
Step 4 creates a role named
SageMakerExecutionRole,

60
00:03:16,250 --> 00:03:20,833
trusted by SageMaker,
with the SageMaker and S3 policies.

61
00:03:21,153 --> 00:03:22,561
Copy its ARN.

62
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:24,208
Why two identities?

63
00:03:24,528 --> 00:03:27,779
Your laptop signs each request
with the user key,

64
00:03:27,779 --> 00:03:31,163
and the endpoint you create
later acts as the role.

65
00:03:31,483 --> 00:03:33,232
The key stays on your laptop.

66
00:03:33,467 --> 00:03:37,830
Step 5, then pause:
create a bucket with a name of your own,

67
00:03:37,830 --> 00:03:41,467
because bucket names are unique
across all of AWS.

68
00:03:41,787 --> 00:03:44,027
Storage bills while files sit in it.

69
00:03:44,347 --> 00:03:47,675
And as the README warns,
never put keys in a script,

70
00:03:47,675 --> 00:03:49,531
and never commit them to Git.

71
00:03:49,767 --> 00:03:53,202
Run Container, step 1: aws configure.

72
00:03:53,522 --> 00:03:54,972
Then come four prompts.

73
00:03:55,292 --> 00:03:56,807
You paste your two keys.

74
00:03:57,127 --> 00:04:02,055
We have none, so we press Enter twice,
then type us-east-1 and json.

75
00:04:02,300 --> 00:04:06,543
Two extras: only the config file exists,
since we typed no keys,

76
00:04:06,543 --> 00:04:10,257
and aws sts get-caller-identity
cannot find credentials.

77
00:04:10,577 --> 00:04:12,689
With your keys, it prints your account.

78
00:04:13,009 --> 00:04:14,738
Pause and make yours print it.

79
00:04:14,984 --> 00:04:18,781
Step 2 offers a second option:
three exports in your shell.

80
00:04:19,101 --> 00:04:21,853
AWS rejects our placeholder key.

81
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:25,640
There is a subtler gap:
the script passes only the region,

82
00:04:25,640 --> 00:04:28,265
the bucket and the role into the container.

83
00:04:28,585 --> 00:04:30,783
So exported keys fall off at the wall.

84
00:04:31,103 --> 00:04:34,004
Use option 1, or add the keys with -e.

85
00:04:37,333 --> 00:04:40,757
Step 3:
edit docker-shell.sh with your bucket,

86
00:04:40,757 --> 00:04:43,584
the ARN of your role, and your region.

87
00:04:43,904 --> 00:04:45,440
Ours are placeholders.

88
00:04:45,833 --> 00:04:48,308
Step 4, chmod, prints nothing.

89
00:04:48,628 --> 00:04:52,404
An extra listing shows
the script was already executable.

90
00:04:52,650 --> 00:04:55,530
Step 5: sh docker-shell.sh.

91
00:04:55,850 --> 00:05:01,299
It pulls the base image for arm64,
then stops at the first RUN line:

92
00:05:01,299 --> 00:05:02,741
exec format error.

93
00:05:03,061 --> 00:05:06,367
Our Intel style laptop
cannot run those programs.

94
00:05:06,616 --> 00:05:08,152
The fix is in the script.

95
00:05:08,472 --> 00:05:11,150
Line 19 is for Apple silicon Macs,

96
00:05:11,150 --> 00:05:14,851
and line 17,
commented out, is the plain build.

97
00:05:15,171 --> 00:05:17,197
Swap them: fix 1, an extra.

98
00:05:17,517 --> 00:05:20,333
On Apple silicon the original line works.

99
00:05:20,653 --> 00:05:23,555
Elsewhere it means slow emulation,
or this error.

100
00:05:23,816 --> 00:05:25,309
Now look at the Pipfile.

101
00:05:25,629 --> 00:05:30,536
TensorFlow is pinned to one version,
but SageMaker says "*": any version.

102
00:05:30,856 --> 00:05:34,195
And no lock is used:
Git ignores the lock file,

103
00:05:34,195 --> 00:05:37,747
and the Dockerfile runs
pipenv lock at build time.

104
00:05:37,983 --> 00:05:39,327
Something to ponder.

105
00:05:39,647 --> 00:05:43,060
The code worked when it was written,
and nobody changed a line.

106
00:05:43,380 --> 00:05:46,388
Why does cli.py fail at import today?

107
00:05:55,233 --> 00:05:58,561
"*" is a constraint,
not a pin, as in lecture 1:

108
00:05:58,561 --> 00:06:02,166
the resolver takes the newest release
that installs.

109
00:06:02,486 --> 00:06:05,964
At the time of recording, on Python 3.9,

110
00:06:05,964 --> 00:06:11,788
that is SageMaker SDK version 3,
with PyTorch and 14 CUDA packages.

111
00:06:12,033 --> 00:06:17,796
Version 3 is a rewritten package,
without the modules cli.py imports:

112
00:06:17,796 --> 00:06:22,806
no tensorflow module, no serializers,
no get_execution_role.

113
00:06:23,126 --> 00:06:24,641
The imports find nothing.

114
00:06:24,883 --> 00:06:28,915
Here, the unpinned build ran 13 minutes,
then ran out of disk.

115
00:06:29,235 --> 00:06:33,032
On your laptop it would finish,
then fail at the first import.

116
00:06:33,266 --> 00:06:36,999
Fix 2, also an extra: change "*" to "<3".

117
00:06:37,319 --> 00:06:40,381
The resolver stops
at the last version 2 release,

118
00:06:40,381 --> 00:06:41,693
and the imports work.

119
00:06:42,013 --> 00:06:43,784
Pause and make both fixes.

120
00:06:45,316 --> 00:06:46,767
Run step 5 again.

121
00:06:47,087 --> 00:06:51,780
It builds in almost 7 minutes,
mostly locking and installing packages.

122
00:06:52,100 --> 00:06:56,324
The AWS check fails without keys,
but the shell still opens.

123
00:06:56,566 --> 00:07:02,767
Inside, extras show Python 3.9,
SageMaker version 2 and TensorFlow 2.18,

124
00:07:02,767 --> 00:07:05,953
and the three values from the script.

125
00:07:06,273 --> 00:07:08,726
Deprecation warnings are not the error.

126
00:07:08,966 --> 00:07:13,680
A small slip: the help text
still says GCS bucket and Vertex AI,

127
00:07:13,680 --> 00:07:15,963
and the test flag does nothing.

128
00:07:16,283 --> 00:07:18,630
The commands are the AWS ones.

129
00:07:18,950 --> 00:07:21,169
The image is about 4 gigabytes.

130
00:07:22,517 --> 00:07:26,328
Before we prepare the model,
a design question from the lecture:

131
00:07:26,328 --> 00:07:28,234
where does preprocessing happen?

132
00:07:28,554 --> 00:07:31,797
In a notebook,
you resize and normalize cell by cell.

133
00:07:32,117 --> 00:07:34,905
A production endpoint
should take the raw photo

134
00:07:34,905 --> 00:07:36,448
and do all of that itself.

135
00:07:37,100 --> 00:07:39,404
In this tutorial, the client does it.

136
00:07:39,724 --> 00:07:43,200
The predict code opens the photo,

137
00:07:43,200 --> 00:07:48,257
converts it to RGB
and resizes it to 224 by 224.

138
00:07:48,577 --> 00:07:53,442
Then it divides
by 255 and sends the numbers as JSON.

139
00:07:53,684 --> 00:07:58,591
An extra check reads the model's front door,
which TensorFlow calls a signature.

140
00:07:58,911 --> 00:08:03,916
It takes floats,
shaped any number by 224 by 224 by 3,

141
00:08:03,916 --> 00:08:06,975
and gives four numbers per photo.

142
00:08:07,295 --> 00:08:09,982
Preprocessing sits on your side of the wire.

143
00:08:10,217 --> 00:08:13,396
The lecture names the risk:
client-side brittleness.

144
00:08:13,716 --> 00:08:17,188
Every client must repeat
those steps exactly,

145
00:08:17,188 --> 00:08:22,590
and a phone app that forgets to divide
by 255 gets confident nonsense.

146
00:08:22,910 --> 00:08:27,348
One copy inside the model
is also easier to roll back and version.

147
00:08:27,584 --> 00:08:30,656
The fix from the lecture sits
in the part 2 trainer.

148
00:08:30,976 --> 00:08:36,266
The preprocess function decodes the JPEG,
converts it to floats, and resizes it.

149
00:08:36,500 --> 00:08:41,110
A function marked as the model's front
door takes a batch of JPEG strings,

150
00:08:41,110 --> 00:08:43,540
prepares each one, and calls the model.

151
00:08:43,860 --> 00:08:45,972
It is saved as the serving_default.

152
00:08:46,292 --> 00:08:48,874
Raw bytes in, one score per class out.

153
00:08:49,117 --> 00:08:50,546
One trap to avoid.

154
00:08:50,866 --> 00:08:55,538
The README says the prepare step converts
the model to the SavedModel format.

155
00:08:55,858 --> 00:08:57,458
That is a change of format,

156
00:08:57,458 --> 00:09:00,125
not the change of signature
from the lecture.

157
00:09:01,467 --> 00:09:05,307
Step 1: python cli.py --prepare.

158
00:09:05,627 --> 00:09:07,312
The README lists four things.

159
00:09:07,632 --> 00:09:12,804
Download the model from GitHub,
convert it to the SavedModel format,

160
00:09:12,804 --> 00:09:16,912
pack it as model.tar.gz,
and upload it to your bucket.

161
00:09:17,232 --> 00:09:20,966
Those are steps 1 and 3 of the lecture
deployment picture.

162
00:09:21,286 --> 00:09:24,656
Step 2, the signature change,
is not in part 1.

163
00:09:25,800 --> 00:09:26,952
Pause and run it.

164
00:09:27,272 --> 00:09:29,215
Ours stops right away:

165
00:09:29,215 --> 00:09:34,781
no credentials, at line 73 of cli.py,
which creates the bucket.

166
00:09:35,101 --> 00:09:39,198
And the artifacts folder does not exist,
so nothing was downloaded.

167
00:09:39,434 --> 00:09:41,137
A small gap in the README:

168
00:09:41,137 --> 00:09:44,871
the code creates the bucket
before it downloads anything,

169
00:09:44,871 --> 00:09:47,007
a step the list does not mention.

170
00:09:47,327 --> 00:09:52,255
With your keys, it creates the bucket,
or reuses yours if it already exists.

171
00:09:52,500 --> 00:09:56,671
To show what comes next, an extra:
the same prepare function,

172
00:09:56,671 --> 00:09:59,817
with S3 replaced by a stub
that sends nothing.

173
00:10:00,137 --> 00:10:03,207
The release zip downloads,
the model is exported,

174
00:10:03,207 --> 00:10:05,087
and the stub stops the upload.

175
00:10:05,407 --> 00:10:07,370
Nothing reaches AWS.

176
00:10:07,867 --> 00:10:09,211
Here is what it made.

177
00:10:09,531 --> 00:10:11,370
A SavedModel is a folder:

178
00:10:11,370 --> 00:10:15,417
the graph and its named entry
points in saved_model.pb,

179
00:10:15,417 --> 00:10:18,214
and the weights in a variables folder.

180
00:10:18,450 --> 00:10:20,953
The code puts it in a folder named 1,

181
00:10:20,953 --> 00:10:25,149
because TensorFlow Serving finds
models by name, then version.

182
00:10:25,469 --> 00:10:27,858
The tarball holds that folder at its top.

183
00:10:28,900 --> 00:10:34,105
An extra git status shows your two edits,
and Git ignores the artifacts folder.

184
00:10:34,425 --> 00:10:39,546
In your account, the tarball now sits in S3,
and storage bills while it exists.

185
00:10:41,883 --> 00:10:43,419
Step 2: deploy.

186
00:10:43,739 --> 00:10:45,979
Remember the three pieces from the start.

187
00:10:46,299 --> 00:10:49,440
In code,
it first describes a TensorFlow model:

188
00:10:49,440 --> 00:10:53,382
the tarball in S3 and the serving container,
plus the role.

189
00:10:53,702 --> 00:10:56,048
Nothing exists in AWS yet.

190
00:10:56,283 --> 00:10:58,950
The lecture uploads
to a model registry here.

191
00:10:59,270 --> 00:11:01,803
Both READMEs say model registry too,

192
00:11:01,803 --> 00:11:05,179
but the code creates a plain model
in SageMaker.

193
00:11:05,416 --> 00:11:07,927
You never write an image name:

194
00:11:07,927 --> 00:11:14,205
the SDK picks the AWS TensorFlow Serving
image from framework version 2.13.

195
00:11:14,525 --> 00:11:17,981
Our model was exported by TensorFlow 2.18.

196
00:11:18,301 --> 00:11:22,611
In step 3, a local check shows
that Serving 2.13 loads it.

197
00:11:22,850 --> 00:11:27,234
Then deploy,
with one instance of type ml.m5.xlarge,

198
00:11:27,234 --> 00:11:29,762
under a name with a timestamp.

199
00:11:30,082 --> 00:11:32,211
That one call creates all three:

200
00:11:32,211 --> 00:11:35,537
the model in SageMaker,
an endpoint configuration,

201
00:11:35,537 --> 00:11:40,663
which names the machine and the model,
and the endpoint, the address you call.

202
00:11:40,900 --> 00:11:44,697
Pause and run python cli.py --deploy.

203
00:11:45,017 --> 00:11:50,031
Ours stops at line 168,
the deploy call: no credentials.

204
00:11:50,351 --> 00:11:53,612
With yours, the README says 5 to 10 minutes,

205
00:11:53,612 --> 00:11:58,798
then it prints the endpoint name
and saves it in endpoint_config.json.

206
00:11:59,033 --> 00:12:02,937
The lecture calls this serverless,
meaning no server for you to manage.

207
00:12:03,257 --> 00:12:04,772
It is still one machine,

208
00:12:04,772 --> 00:12:07,865
billing around the clock,
even with zero traffic.

209
00:12:08,185 --> 00:12:11,562
Unlike Cloud Run in lecture 14,
this endpoint,

210
00:12:11,562 --> 00:12:15,012
as the code creates it,
does not scale to zero.

211
00:12:15,250 --> 00:12:17,383
The lecture compares two ways to serve.

212
00:12:17,703 --> 00:12:21,047
A model endpoint deploys
a trained model directly,

213
00:12:21,047 --> 00:12:24,658
with versioning,
A/B testing and monitoring available.

214
00:12:24,978 --> 00:12:28,544
A general container service
like Cloud Run runs anything,

215
00:12:28,544 --> 00:12:30,546
and you build the rest yourself.

216
00:12:30,783 --> 00:12:34,468
Its rule of thumb:
an endpoint to serve a trained model,

217
00:12:34,468 --> 00:12:38,612
a container service
when the model is one part of a larger app.

218
00:12:38,932 --> 00:12:40,895
Here, those features go unused.

219
00:12:42,233 --> 00:12:45,966
Step 3: python cli.py --predict.

220
00:12:46,286 --> 00:12:51,470
Ours finds no endpoint_config.json
and falls back to a default name.

221
00:12:51,790 --> 00:12:56,804
It picks five photos and fails five times:
Unable to locate credentials.

222
00:12:57,124 --> 00:12:58,596
Pause and run yours.

223
00:12:58,833 --> 00:13:00,753
Each try would send one photo.

224
00:13:01,073 --> 00:13:07,217
The client resizes it to 224 by 224,
with 3 color channels.

225
00:13:07,537 --> 00:13:10,993
How many numbers travel
to the endpoint for that one photo?

226
00:13:19,833 --> 00:13:27,513
224 times 224 times 3: 150,528 floats.

227
00:13:27,833 --> 00:13:32,569
Python writes each one as text in JSON,
so a few megabytes per photo.

228
00:13:32,889 --> 00:13:36,409
A signature
that takes bytes would send the JPEG itself.

229
00:13:36,650 --> 00:13:39,317
The answer is a list of four probabilities.

230
00:13:39,637 --> 00:13:43,951
The code takes the largest with argmax
and looks it up:

231
00:13:43,951 --> 00:13:47,167
0 parmigiano, 1 gruyere, 2 brie, 3 gouda.

232
00:13:47,416 --> 00:13:48,988
To see a real answer,

233
00:13:48,988 --> 00:13:52,429
we use an extra local stand-in,
not SageMaker.

234
00:13:52,749 --> 00:13:56,420
The AWS serving image
runs TensorFlow Serving,

235
00:13:56,420 --> 00:14:01,048
so we started the public
Serving 2.13 image on our export.

236
00:14:01,368 --> 00:14:02,862
It loads version 1.

237
00:14:03,100 --> 00:14:06,961
Then the same predict function
sends the same JSON to it.

238
00:14:07,281 --> 00:14:08,531
Brie comes back brie,

239
00:14:08,531 --> 00:14:11,505
and gruyere comes back gruyere,
four numbers each.

240
00:14:11,750 --> 00:14:15,249
And parmigiano comes back brie,
at 98 percent.

241
00:14:15,569 --> 00:14:18,385
That is a real mistake
of the released model.

242
00:14:18,705 --> 00:14:22,864
Its own metrics file reports
about 72 percent accuracy.

243
00:14:23,100 --> 00:14:24,615
Now the opening puzzle.

244
00:14:24,935 --> 00:14:29,685
Same code, different artifact:
the README output came from an older model,

245
00:14:29,685 --> 00:14:32,444
with three classes and another label order.

246
00:14:32,764 --> 00:14:37,308
A small gap: expect four numbers,
and index 0 is parmigiano.

247
00:14:37,550 --> 00:14:40,046
An extra command removes the stand-in.

248
00:14:40,366 --> 00:14:42,328
It never touched AWS.

249
00:14:43,567 --> 00:14:47,455
The lecture warns
that an endpoint costs money while it is up,

250
00:14:47,455 --> 00:14:49,839
and the slides shout one word: delete.

251
00:14:50,159 --> 00:14:51,844
The README gives three ways.

252
00:14:52,164 --> 00:14:56,730
Any one stops the endpoint,
but only the third also removes the tarball.

253
00:14:57,050 --> 00:15:01,466
Way 1, the console:
SageMaker, Endpoints, select yours, Delete.

254
00:15:01,767 --> 00:15:04,924
Way 2 gives three AWS CLI commands.

255
00:15:05,244 --> 00:15:08,820
Typed exactly as written,
the first one is a shell error:

256
00:15:08,820 --> 00:15:13,778
the angle brackets are placeholders,
and the shell takes them as a redirection.

257
00:15:14,017 --> 00:15:16,961
A small gap:
type your own names, without brackets.

258
00:15:17,281 --> 00:15:19,777
Ours then fails: no credentials.

259
00:15:20,097 --> 00:15:24,790
Delete in this order: the endpoint,
then its configuration, then the model.

260
00:15:26,567 --> 00:15:32,626
Way 3: python cli.py --delete stops
at its first SageMaker call.

261
00:15:32,946 --> 00:15:34,098
Pause and run it.

262
00:15:34,418 --> 00:15:38,279
With keys, it deletes the endpoint
named in the JSON file.

263
00:15:38,599 --> 00:15:41,979
Then come matching configurations
and the model,

264
00:15:41,979 --> 00:15:45,148
by naming convention,
then the tarball in S3.

265
00:15:45,384 --> 00:15:49,053
Each deploy makes a new endpoint
with a new timestamp,

266
00:15:49,053 --> 00:15:51,976
but the JSON file keeps only the last name.

267
00:15:52,296 --> 00:15:54,813
You ran deploy twice, then delete once.

268
00:15:55,133 --> 00:15:56,840
How many endpoints still bill?

269
00:16:03,684 --> 00:16:07,737
One. Delete removes only
the endpoint the file names.

270
00:16:08,057 --> 00:16:09,721
The older one keeps running.

271
00:16:10,041 --> 00:16:14,798
Check with aws sagemaker list-endpoints,
and confirm in the console.

272
00:16:15,034 --> 00:16:16,250
That is part 1.

273
00:16:16,570 --> 00:16:19,791
You ran four commands
by hand to deploy one model.

274
00:16:20,111 --> 00:16:22,735
The Cheese App has four stages before that.

275
00:16:23,055 --> 00:16:26,170
Who runs all of them,
in the right order, every time?

276
00:16:27,217 --> 00:16:28,217
Part 2.

277
00:16:28,454 --> 00:16:33,617
The lecture draws the problem: four stages,
each run by a person at a terminal.

278
00:16:33,937 --> 00:16:35,772
How do we execute these steps?

279
00:16:36,092 --> 00:16:37,905
And can we automate this flow?

280
00:16:38,150 --> 00:16:40,198
The answer is an orchestrator.

281
00:16:40,518 --> 00:16:44,166
The lecture uses Vertex AI Pipelines,
on Kubeflow.

282
00:16:44,486 --> 00:16:50,204
This tutorial uses SageMaker Pipelines,
the same idea of managed steps in a graph.

283
00:16:50,450 --> 00:16:53,308
Workflow management runs the steps in order,

284
00:16:53,308 --> 00:16:57,597
makes a run repeatable,
gives each step its machine, and shows it.

285
00:16:57,917 --> 00:16:59,730
This repo covers those four.

286
00:17:00,050 --> 00:17:02,397
Scheduling waits for lecture 23.

287
00:17:02,717 --> 00:17:05,618
And it is managed:
no cluster for you to run.

288
00:17:05,867 --> 00:17:09,366
Setup, step 1: clone ml-workflow-aws.

289
00:17:09,686 --> 00:17:11,542
Our git clone is extra again.

290
00:17:11,862 --> 00:17:14,922
An extra tree
shows four folders under source:

291
00:17:14,922 --> 00:17:16,918
three stages and the workflow.

292
00:17:17,800 --> 00:17:22,139
Steps 2 to 5 are console work:
quick setup for SageMaker AI,

293
00:17:22,139 --> 00:17:26,696
and a Unified Studio domain,
where you will watch the pipeline.

294
00:17:27,016 --> 00:17:30,563
Then your bucket,
and a user named ml-workflow-user,

295
00:17:30,563 --> 00:17:31,859
with four policies.

296
00:17:33,100 --> 00:17:37,559
Create an access key for the command line,
and download the .csv file.

297
00:17:37,879 --> 00:17:40,910
Quick setup and the Studio domain
stay in your account:

298
00:17:40,910 --> 00:17:42,508
check them when you clean up.

299
00:17:42,750 --> 00:17:47,817
Steps 6 and 7:
install the AWS CLI, then aws configure,

300
00:17:47,817 --> 00:17:51,411
and paste the two values from the .csv.

301
00:17:51,731 --> 00:17:55,598
From here on,
we typed obviously fake placeholders,

302
00:17:55,598 --> 00:17:58,025
so AWS answers with real errors.

303
00:17:58,267 --> 00:18:00,550
Step 8 tests the configuration.

304
00:18:00,870 --> 00:18:04,795
AWS itself rejects the fake token
and the fake key.

305
00:18:05,115 --> 00:18:07,952
With yours,
these print your account and your buckets.

306
00:18:08,272 --> 00:18:10,022
Don't go on until they do.

307
00:18:10,267 --> 00:18:14,901
Step 9 creates two roles:
CheeseAppSageMakerExecutionRole,

308
00:18:14,901 --> 00:18:18,736
for every job,
and CheeseAppLambdaExecutionRole.

309
00:18:19,056 --> 00:18:22,206
The code builds each ARN
from your account number

310
00:18:22,206 --> 00:18:23,749
and the exact role name,

311
00:18:23,749 --> 00:18:26,160
so spell them exactly, and pause here.

312
00:18:27,733 --> 00:18:29,568
Data collector, step 1.

313
00:18:29,888 --> 00:18:31,082
A small gap first:

314
00:18:31,082 --> 00:18:34,843
all four part 2 scripts name the bucket
from the README,

315
00:18:34,843 --> 00:18:36,522
so put yours in each one.

316
00:18:36,842 --> 00:18:40,704
Then, in this folder,
run sh docker-shell.sh.

317
00:18:41,024 --> 00:18:45,418
Without keys, the script stops itself:
AWS not configured.

318
00:18:45,666 --> 00:18:49,549
With keys, it builds in 40 seconds
and opens a root shell.

319
00:18:49,869 --> 00:18:50,869
Two notes.

320
00:18:51,127 --> 00:18:55,906
This script builds for amd64,
which Apple silicon emulates.

321
00:18:56,226 --> 00:18:59,197
And on Ubuntu or Debian, sh is dash,

322
00:18:59,197 --> 00:19:03,736
which skips the key check,
so run bash docker-shell.sh.

323
00:19:03,983 --> 00:19:04,983
Why a shell?

324
00:19:05,263 --> 00:19:07,290
The entry point script has a switch.

325
00:19:07,610 --> 00:19:11,898
No arguments: it opens bash,
development mode, where you are now.

326
00:19:12,218 --> 00:19:15,546
Arguments: it runs them and exits,
production mode.

327
00:19:15,866 --> 00:19:18,362
The lecture calls that a callable container.

328
00:19:18,600 --> 00:19:22,547
Step 2: the search command,
four cheeses, ten images each.

329
00:19:22,867 --> 00:19:26,255
It scrapes the web,
writes four folders, zips them,

330
00:19:26,255 --> 00:19:28,115
and tries to upload raw.zip.

331
00:19:28,435 --> 00:19:30,802
AWS rejects our fake key.

332
00:19:31,050 --> 00:19:33,738
Step 3, check your bucket, cannot pass here.

333
00:19:34,058 --> 00:19:37,109
An extra listing shows 40 images on disk.

334
00:19:37,429 --> 00:19:41,844
On the laptop, a root-owned persistent
folder appeared beside the clones.

335
00:19:42,083 --> 00:19:44,096
Data processor, step 1:

336
00:19:44,096 --> 00:19:48,910
the arm64 line fails again,
and the same swap fixes it.

337
00:19:49,450 --> 00:19:53,013
Step 2: python cli.py --clean.

338
00:19:53,333 --> 00:19:55,967
A duplicate finder checks each label,

339
00:19:55,967 --> 00:20:00,309
finds none among these 40,
and the upload of clean.zip fails.

340
00:20:00,629 --> 00:20:01,780
Pause and run it.

341
00:20:02,016 --> 00:20:05,771
Steps 3 and 4:
check your bucket, then --prepare.

342
00:20:06,091 --> 00:20:11,397
All 40 images split into 32 for training
and 8 for validation,

343
00:20:11,397 --> 00:20:13,707
in two real TFRecord files.

344
00:20:14,027 --> 00:20:15,520
The upload fails again.

345
00:20:15,840 --> 00:20:18,166
Step 5 finds them only on disk.

346
00:20:18,650 --> 00:20:21,316
The duty cards from the lecture match:

347
00:20:21,316 --> 00:20:25,946
download, then verify, remove duplicates
and convert to TFRecords.

348
00:20:26,266 --> 00:20:29,132
And the four labels
are just the folder names:

349
00:20:29,132 --> 00:20:31,748
your search queries, so type them exactly.

350
00:20:34,050 --> 00:20:35,821
Model training, step 1.

351
00:20:36,141 --> 00:20:38,999
Same arm64 line, same swap.

352
00:20:39,319 --> 00:20:42,314
This image installs from its committed lock,

353
00:20:42,314 --> 00:20:45,378
with SageMaker version 2,
so it needs no pin.

354
00:20:45,617 --> 00:20:47,366
Step 2, local training.

355
00:20:47,686 --> 00:20:52,905
TensorFlow 2.20 on the CPU finds
the TFRecords from the processor,

356
00:20:52,905 --> 00:20:54,961
through the shared folder.

357
00:20:55,281 --> 00:20:57,649
Look at the last layer: three outputs.

358
00:20:57,969 --> 00:20:59,206
One epoch runs.

359
00:20:59,526 --> 00:21:01,531
Its accuracy means nothing yet.

360
00:21:01,767 --> 00:21:06,226
Then saving fails:
Permission denied, /persistent/model.

361
00:21:06,546 --> 00:21:08,551
Pause here for two small fixes.

362
00:21:08,800 --> 00:21:11,250
First, line 68 says three classes,

363
00:21:11,250 --> 00:21:15,285
while the data has four labels,
so gouda gets no target.

364
00:21:15,605 --> 00:21:16,864
Change it to 4.

365
00:21:17,100 --> 00:21:21,367
Second, on Linux, Docker created
the persistent folder as root,

366
00:21:21,367 --> 00:21:24,076
and this container runs as the app user.

367
00:21:24,396 --> 00:21:28,983
Exit, and on your laptop change its owner
with the chown command on the card.

368
00:21:29,303 --> 00:21:34,551
On a fresh clone, create the folder yourself
before the first docker-shell.sh.

369
00:21:34,871 --> 00:21:36,428
Then run step 2 again.

370
00:21:36,748 --> 00:21:39,223
Docker Desktop on a Mac avoids this.

371
00:21:39,467 --> 00:21:45,167
It would write to /persistent/model/1:
the persistent folder on your laptop,

372
00:21:45,167 --> 00:21:49,067
or the SageMaker model folder
inside a training job.

373
00:21:49,317 --> 00:21:53,645
Just before the save sits
the signature change from the lecture:

374
00:21:53,645 --> 00:21:56,485
decode, convert, resize, inside the model.

375
00:21:56,805 --> 00:22:00,602
A small slip:
the README gives this job to model deploy.

376
00:22:00,922 --> 00:22:02,181
The trainer does it.

377
00:22:02,417 --> 00:22:08,518
Step 3: sh package-trainer.sh packs
the package folder and copies it to S3.

378
00:22:08,838 --> 00:22:10,396
The tar file is real.

379
00:22:10,716 --> 00:22:12,678
The upload fails on our fake key.

380
00:22:12,917 --> 00:22:16,437
Step 4: python cli.py --train.

381
00:22:16,757 --> 00:22:21,450
It asks AWS who you are,
to build the role ARN, and stops there.

382
00:22:21,770 --> 00:22:26,176
With keys,
it starts a training job on ml.m5.xlarge,

383
00:22:26,176 --> 00:22:27,871
and returns at once.

384
00:22:28,117 --> 00:22:32,661
Step 5: watch it in the console,
under Training, then Training jobs.

385
00:22:32,981 --> 00:22:34,623
Its name starts with cheese.

386
00:22:35,967 --> 00:22:37,034
Build and push.

387
00:22:37,354 --> 00:22:40,298
The README says
this step has already been done for you.

388
00:22:40,618 --> 00:22:44,623
A small gap: the workflow code
pulls both images from the ECR

389
00:22:44,623 --> 00:22:49,023
registry of your own account,
so you push them yourself, once each.

390
00:22:49,267 --> 00:22:53,502
That is the second step the lecture
gives for your own containers:

391
00:22:53,502 --> 00:22:57,160
push the images
to a registry the platform can pull from.

392
00:22:57,400 --> 00:23:01,496
Steps 1 to 3 go through Docker Hub:
log in, build, push.

393
00:23:01,816 --> 00:23:06,403
The Mac line builds amd64,
because SageMaker runs that.

394
00:23:07,050 --> 00:23:09,055
Step 4 sets five variables.

395
00:23:09,375 --> 00:23:13,118
Pasted exactly as written,
the first line prints two errors,

396
00:23:13,118 --> 00:23:17,610
because export rejects the bracket note,
though the region is still set.

397
00:23:17,930 --> 00:23:22,432
The account lookup fails on our fake key,
and the URI starts with a dot.

398
00:23:22,667 --> 00:23:25,334
So paste only the value, us-east-1.

399
00:23:25,654 --> 00:23:29,702
And if your URI starts with a dot,
the account lookup failed:

400
00:23:29,702 --> 00:23:31,030
fix your keys first.

401
00:23:31,267 --> 00:23:33,016
Step 5 pulls your image.

402
00:23:33,336 --> 00:23:38,582
Step 6 creates the repository,
and the || true at the end hides the error:

403
00:23:38,582 --> 00:23:40,568
the command exits with zero.

404
00:23:40,817 --> 00:23:44,996
Steps 7 to 11 log Docker in to ECR,
tag the image,

405
00:23:44,996 --> 00:23:48,006
push it, list it, and print the URI.

406
00:23:48,326 --> 00:23:51,334
Tag only adds a second name
to the same image.

407
00:23:51,654 --> 00:23:54,962
Run them on your laptop,
not in the workflow container,

408
00:23:54,962 --> 00:23:56,646
which has no docker command.

409
00:23:56,966 --> 00:23:59,036
Then repeat for the processor image.

410
00:24:03,050 --> 00:24:06,890
Automate the data collector, step 1,
in the workflow folder.

411
00:24:07,210 --> 00:24:11,946
Before you run sh docker-shell.sh,
the "*" problem returns.

412
00:24:12,266 --> 00:24:16,511
A lock is committed,
but the Dockerfile copies only the Pipfile,

413
00:24:16,511 --> 00:24:18,965
so the build would resolve version 3.

414
00:24:19,200 --> 00:24:21,120
The checks show the imports failing.

415
00:24:21,440 --> 00:24:24,277
So make the same fix first: "<3".

416
00:24:30,333 --> 00:24:32,978
Now run sh docker-shell.sh.

417
00:24:33,298 --> 00:24:37,032
The banner shows region and bucket,
and the who am I check fails.

418
00:24:37,352 --> 00:24:41,448
Extras: SageMaker version 2 now,
and no docker command.

419
00:24:41,768 --> 00:24:45,437
The socket is mounted,
but no docker program is inside.

420
00:24:45,683 --> 00:24:50,163
From here, the SDK is your remote control,
as the lecture puts it.

421
00:24:50,483 --> 00:24:53,683
Your Python asks, and AWS machines run.

422
00:24:54,050 --> 00:24:58,125
Step 2: python cli.py --data_collector.

423
00:24:58,445 --> 00:24:59,761
It stops at line 32,

424
00:24:59,761 --> 00:25:02,919
a lookup of your account
at the top of the file,

425
00:25:02,919 --> 00:25:04,994
before the function even starts.

426
00:25:05,314 --> 00:25:09,346
Every flag of this CLI needs a working key
at import time.

427
00:25:09,583 --> 00:25:15,492
With keys, it builds a processor
from your ECR image, on one ml.t3.medium.

428
00:25:15,812 --> 00:25:20,164
Its entry point is python3
with the collector cli.py.

429
00:25:20,484 --> 00:25:23,345
Then it runs the job
with the search arguments,

430
00:25:23,345 --> 00:25:25,050
and waits, showing the logs.

431
00:25:25,317 --> 00:25:26,768
This is production mode.

432
00:25:27,088 --> 00:25:30,757
SageMaker puts its own command
in front of the arguments.

433
00:25:31,077 --> 00:25:35,834
Same image, same cli.py,
on a machine you never log in to.

434
00:25:36,154 --> 00:25:38,778
The job acts as the role, not with your key.

435
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:43,776
Step 3: watch it under Processing,
then Processing jobs.

436
00:25:45,750 --> 00:25:47,243
Now the whole pipeline.

437
00:25:47,563 --> 00:25:53,899
The slides write steps in the Kubeflow DSL,
wired by .output and compiled to YAML.

438
00:25:54,219 --> 00:25:58,529
Here: SageMaker step classes,
property references and JSON.

439
00:25:59,467 --> 00:26:01,600
First, caching, for 30 days.

440
00:26:01,920 --> 00:26:05,662
The first step is a processing step
named DataCollector,

441
00:26:05,662 --> 00:26:08,000
with one declared output, raw-data.

442
00:26:08,250 --> 00:26:13,687
The second step, the processor,
takes as input a property of the collector:

443
00:26:13,687 --> 00:26:15,717
its raw-data output, S3 URI.

444
00:26:16,037 --> 00:26:18,853
Not a string: a reference,
and it draws an edge.

445
00:26:19,617 --> 00:26:23,876
Training takes the TFRecords
output of the processor the same way,

446
00:26:23,876 --> 00:26:27,297
and the model step takes
the trained model artifacts.

447
00:26:27,617 --> 00:26:29,771
Each reference draws one more edge.

448
00:26:30,017 --> 00:26:35,222
Last, the Lambda step named DeployModel
takes the model name from the model step.

449
00:26:35,542 --> 00:26:38,065
Then the pipeline lists all five steps,

450
00:26:38,065 --> 00:26:43,371
creates the deploy Lambda from your laptop,
upserts its definition, and starts it.

451
00:26:44,167 --> 00:26:45,511
Something to ponder.

452
00:26:45,831 --> 00:26:48,391
Reverse the list of steps
in the Pipeline call.

453
00:26:48,711 --> 00:26:50,204
Does the run order change?

454
00:26:59,050 --> 00:27:04,981
No. The order comes from the references:
each input names the output of another step.

455
00:27:05,301 --> 00:27:07,967
The list is a set of steps, not a schedule.

456
00:27:08,217 --> 00:27:13,785
An extra, built offline and never sent:
the JSON definition from the SDK.

457
00:27:14,105 --> 00:27:17,497
Five steps, each edge a get
on a property of another step.

458
00:27:17,817 --> 00:27:19,139
Upsert sends this.

459
00:27:20,317 --> 00:27:22,088
The lecture draws four steps.

460
00:27:22,408 --> 00:27:24,264
Here, deploy splits in two.

461
00:27:24,584 --> 00:27:27,853
CreateModel makes the model, and a Lambda,

462
00:27:27,853 --> 00:27:33,223
a small function AWS runs
on demand, creates or updates the endpoint.

463
00:27:34,467 --> 00:27:35,896
One honest caveat.

464
00:27:36,216 --> 00:27:37,899
The edges set the order,

465
00:27:37,899 --> 00:27:41,825
but files also travel
by fixed names through the bucket:

466
00:27:41,825 --> 00:27:44,387
raw.zip, clean.zip and tfrecords.zip.

467
00:27:44,707 --> 00:27:47,156
We could not check, without an account,

468
00:27:47,156 --> 00:27:50,232
how the training job finds its data
on SageMaker.

469
00:27:50,467 --> 00:27:56,654
Step 1: python cli.py --pipeline
stops at the same line 32.

470
00:27:56,974 --> 00:27:59,726
With keys,
it prints that the pipeline started.

471
00:28:00,046 --> 00:28:04,374
If instead upsert fails
on the Lambda runtime,

472
00:28:04,374 --> 00:28:09,454
add runtime="python3.12"
to the Lambda call, line 297.

473
00:28:09,774 --> 00:28:11,601
Step 2: in Unified Studio,

474
00:28:11,601 --> 00:28:15,256
under Build, ML Pipelines,
you would see this graph.

475
00:28:15,500 --> 00:28:19,848
If every step succeeds,
caching skips unchanged steps for 30 days,

476
00:28:19,848 --> 00:28:22,220
so the collector won't scrape again.

477
00:28:22,540 --> 00:28:26,189
And a green DeployModel
only means the Lambda asked:

478
00:28:26,189 --> 00:28:28,855
wait until the endpoint is in service.

479
00:28:29,100 --> 00:28:32,279
Step 3 tests each component
with its own flag.

480
00:28:32,599 --> 00:28:34,796
Here, all four stop at that line.

481
00:28:35,116 --> 00:28:38,295
With keys,
model deploy makes yet another endpoint.

482
00:28:39,633 --> 00:28:41,233
Before you leave, clean up.

483
00:28:41,553 --> 00:28:44,239
List your endpoints and delete every one:

484
00:28:44,239 --> 00:28:48,038
the timestamped ones from part 1,
and cheese-app-endpoint.

485
00:28:48,358 --> 00:28:51,153
Then delete their configurations and models.

486
00:28:51,400 --> 00:28:53,405
Empty and delete both buckets.

487
00:28:53,725 --> 00:28:56,014
Delete the two ECR repositories,

488
00:28:56,014 --> 00:28:59,805
the deploy Lambda functions,
and the cheese pipeline.

489
00:29:00,050 --> 00:29:05,298
Deactivate and delete the access keys,
and the users and roles you no longer need.

490
00:29:05,618 --> 00:29:11,442
Finally, check the SageMaker AI domain
and the Unified Studio domain you created.

491
00:29:14,283 --> 00:29:16,340
The lecture ends on a factory:

492
00:29:16,340 --> 00:29:20,384
your Python is the recipe,
and SageMaker Pipelines runs it.

493
00:29:20,704 --> 00:29:24,694
Today's pictures:
a key on the laptop and a role in the cloud.

494
00:29:25,014 --> 00:29:26,486
A star is not a pin.

495
00:29:26,806 --> 00:29:29,835
Preprocessing sits in the client
or in the model.

496
00:29:30,155 --> 00:29:34,656
Three pieces stand behind an endpoint,
and references draw the edges.

497
00:29:34,900 --> 00:29:37,183
Under it, part 1 in four commands.

498
00:29:37,503 --> 00:29:39,060
Prepare packs the model,

499
00:29:39,060 --> 00:29:42,431
deploy builds the model,
configuration and endpoint.

500
00:29:42,751 --> 00:29:46,100
Predict sends the numbers,
and delete turns off the meter.

501
00:29:46,350 --> 00:29:47,459
Test yourself.

502
00:29:47,779 --> 00:29:53,027
Your laptop and the endpoint both reach S3:
which identity does each use?

503
00:29:53,347 --> 00:29:57,422
And why do the option 2 exports
never reach the part 1 container?

504
00:29:57,666 --> 00:29:59,821
Name the three things deploy creates.

505
00:30:00,141 --> 00:30:04,002
Which one is the address,
and which one bills while nobody calls it?

506
00:30:04,322 --> 00:30:06,724
Where does preprocessing sit in part 1,

507
00:30:06,724 --> 00:30:10,296
and in the part 2 trainer,
and why move it into the model?

508
00:30:10,533 --> 00:30:13,200
Which lines make training wait
for the processor?

509
00:30:13,520 --> 00:30:18,512
Which two one-line fixes did today need,
and which would an Apple silicon Mac skip?

510
00:30:18,832 --> 00:30:21,245
The answers are in the exercises,

511
00:30:21,245 --> 00:30:24,826
and lecture 23 triggers the pipeline
from GitHub.

